# Rust Coding Conventions

## Error Handling

- Use `thiserror` for error type definitions in `src/error.rs`
- Use `anyhow` for ad-hoc error contexts in non-library code
- Never use `unwrap()` in tool implementations -- return `CallToolResult::error(...)` instead
- Distinguish between recoverable errors (return to caller) and fatal errors (log + propagate)

## Async Patterns

- All async code runs on Tokio multi-threaded runtime
- Use `tokio::sync::Mutex` (not `std::sync::Mutex`) when locks cross `.await` points
- When `.clone()` is needed for async moves, comment why
- Use `Arc<T>` for shared state; prefer channels over `Arc<Mutex<T>>` when viable

## Ownership

- Prefer borrowing (`&self`, `&str`, `&[T]`) over cloning
- Follow the existing `Arc<Config>` pattern on `MatrixMcpServer` for shared state

## Quality

- Run `cargo clippy` (warn level) before committing
- Run `cargo fmt` (rustfmt defaults) for formatting
- Run `cargo test` to verify nothing breaks
- Release profile uses LTO + strip + codegen-units=1

## Dependencies

- Don't add dependencies without justification
- Check if existing crates (serde, tokio, axum) already provide needed functionality
- Pin versions in Cargo.toml to avoid surprise breakage
